Improved scheduling question: “Beyond the written cooking times, which hidden states and human gates must line up: oven preheat complete before the braise begins; the tart shell chilled at least 30 but no more than 120 minutes and assembled before the ribs leave the oven; the soup finished and held warm until the guests are seated; the ribs rested, skimmed, and reduced before plating; and the table clearances that determine when the next course starts?”

Answer and track/trigger consequences: the oven line is preheat → braise → tart bake, because both braise and tart need the only oven. The tart-prep line runs during the braise: pastry is rolled after the stovetop rib steps, chilled, then apples are sliced and arranged; the tart bake is compounded to start only when the ribs leave the oven *and* the tart is assembled. The soup line is triggered two hours into the braise but also after the tart slice step, so the single cook is not in two places at once. The soup is held warm as an indefinite step ending at the seating gate, so the soup service starts at 19:30. The service steps after seating are modeled as clock gates only where deterministic and indefinite gates where the main course length is unknown: plate ribs ten minutes after soup service/clearance, let the main run as long as it runs, and slice/serve the tart twenty minutes after main plates return.

